ield/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "98bb0766-112b-5f2d-9ce2-f91491bdb354" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{6}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{6}}={x}^{6/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{6}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{6/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5+1}}{(\\frac{6}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{11/5}}{(\\frac{11}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{11/5}}{11}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{11/5}}{11}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{11/5}}{11})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{11}\\cdot \\frac{11}{5}{x}^{11/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{6/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b264e9f-1b95-5da0-8a5d-53d43f387515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b264e9f-1b95-5da0-8a5d-53d43f387515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503898} :answer-field/id #uuid "7639bdc5-e09a-59de-9a6d-7760ce0dd6a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a04ec978-e44a-5fc0-941a-3437fa7bf03c" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}={x}^{9/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{9}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{9/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5+1}}{(\\frac{9}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{14/5}}{(\\frac{14}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{14/5}}{14})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{14}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{14/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{14}\\cdot \\frac{14}{5}{x}^{14/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{9/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a07fc94f-5b6d-5ea9-93c2-7e82f2229b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a07fc94f-5b6d-5ea9-93c2-7e82f2229b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503901} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfe4c120-0640-5e64-a81c-759b876005de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a26b65ff-d0b9-55fd-88b5-aeb6bb366617" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}={x}^{8/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{8}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{8/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8/5+1}}{(\\frac{8}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{13/5}}{(\\frac{13}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{13/5}}{13})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{13}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{13/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{5}{x}^{13/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{8/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9b9a59b-1b30-5f5a-96ca-ff44ec806088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9b9a59b-1b30-5f5a-96ca-ff44ec806088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503915} :answer-field/id #uuid "63f7164e-c9db-55ed-a2c8-afeccd4f032f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3c79b12-9c14-5549-a389-57292bb076aa" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{4/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{(\\frac{9}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}{x}^{9/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{4/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d457834a-752e-5c58-903b-17caad36936f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d457834a-752e-5c58-903b-17caad36936f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503912} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2aee605-eea6-5ac3-a7aa-7faf947e835f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab9f77bb-3e23-5176-a3ef-3da256c8008e" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{3}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{(\\frac{5}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181054c9-dbd9-5b5f-a504-255307d12709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "181054c9-dbd9-5b5f-a504-255307d12709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503880} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4ba04d-d537-57d4-bc2d-b413bfc38584" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1ab8645-2279-57cf-bef0-772d2fda5a19" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{4/3}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{4/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3+1}}{(\\frac{4}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/3}}{(\\frac{7}{3})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{7/3}}{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}{x}^{7/3-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{4/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b19525f-2038-5110-92f7-e19997cc0bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b19525f-2038-5110-92f7-e19997cc0bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503885} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbecae76-7205-578e-830e-6ad24ef696f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e61be54d-6f89-5777-b645-69438909a48d" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{7}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2+1}}{(\\frac{7}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/2}}{(\\frac{9}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{9/2}}{9})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}{x}^{9/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{7/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb7a833-25cc-55e9-bcd4-a500bc7129f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cb7a833-25cc-55e9-bcd4-a500bc7129f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504138} :answer-field/id #uuid "915f88a5-971a-5c31-97f8-4339c3567b6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "174ba687-7b9b-5ccb-9e0f-e16e772aa04c" :question/math-academy-id "q-346221" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\pi }{6n}$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi /6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi /6-0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(\\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01384154-a073-56e0-9faf-43be4f562891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504032} {:answer/id #uuid "08ee82a2-784d-5e9b-adf7-eadff82d9072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504048} {:answer/id #uuid "24a0b4c2-0c8c-5103-9f4a-eed2189e8fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504095} {:answer/id #uuid "394f7ad5-b9aa-5163-b7f9-a244a184d786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504132} {:answer/id #uuid "b05ef87e-9cad-533d-bf6e-91d974d3d93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01384154-a073-56e0-9faf-43be4f562891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504032} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0150772-06cd-545e-9657-56eec3250d1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "215b8d43-0fa8-5bc5-9a2a-4cbd17fc3abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107916" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{cx+4}, & x\\ne -1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{27}, & x=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1,$ what is the value of $c?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{cx+4}={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4-c} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ 4-c & =3 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027fa49b-eaf8-5d5e-a730-1b6f7fa916e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504035} {:answer/id #uuid "5aaccb67-ceda-5dd0-8a10-f61a5a273841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504190} {:answer/id #uuid "b036582e-39f9-5e50-b0db-d7985a363b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504337} {:answer/id #uuid "d3837867-f45b-547f-9d74-b1e884fcfe7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504395} {:answer/id #uuid "de534e5e-3fce-5121-9f25-e5c2f538099b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "027fa49b-eaf8-5d5e-a730-1b6f7fa916e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504035} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b2d790-750c-54c8-8acc-4cf1f5c0b6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "275cbf3f-d183-51d1-83d2-3cab6277b499" :question/math-academy-id "q-152787" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ directly into the limit, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/3}(12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\pi ) & =12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})+\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac13e6e-13ad-59cb-b226-1c8376af83ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ac13e6e-13ad-59cb-b226-1c8376af83ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504080} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fcf9895-9e04-5cf4-b7d2-beef16232060" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28d2bedc-f4b0-58dc-80a4-886e3d716859" :question/math-academy-id "q-340259" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{x+3}, & x\\ne -3 \\\\ 2, & x=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if  $-\\frac{3}{x+3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{3}{x+3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48610f07da2103a0e1416318c1c70da426d8c845f0870387836098287d83efb8.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "359a0b0c-50e8-5345-975b-7a3b5f1bf014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504123} {:answer/id #uuid "45c11c78-2d68-5904-8031-3d3a0242f7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504158} {:answer/id #uuid "ce74393d-eae8-577c-a62b-226565601386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504382} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0f3d27-f2c4-53e6-a0dd-86a7157d57b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504416} {:answer/id #uuid "ee53d443-4dcf-58ab-8684-f164d88801ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce74393d-eae8-577c-a62b-226565601386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504382} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0c67064-cabf-594b-8138-f2a53ecff042" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "328730e5-b32c-5f55-aef8-1fdf6d6f55ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-94622" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $Y$ if the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e167e3-3c1a-5a94-aedd-937cca7aa527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504100} {:answer/id #uuid "4776454d-1433-55b0-a3f1-6744fa92a4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504161} {:answer/id #uuid "869d1c4e-d590-57e1-8c7b-108e1a80a41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504267} {:answer/id #uuid "a85269ed-a160-5cdf-ba28-b8d04e6cc4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504325} {:answer/id #uuid "e5adc569-5d12-5e17-a6da-903c09507f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27e167e3-3c1a-5a94-aedd-937cca7aa527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504100} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc30e01e-9aef-5e28-9a4c-abb1934ac9fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "445c4dee-b549-5e10-93bb-06205e66703c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106854" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07850059-ddb8-59d8-9ab0-8177d5206ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504046} {:answer/id #uuid "4d11d421-27fe-5c53-897b-17c35864bd3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504171} {:answer/id #uuid "c141ccd2-2281-55be-a7bc-e600a17abbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504361} {:answer/id #uuid "c969f3b4-9799-57d4-a8f4-6cf2a9b701dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504372} {:answer/id #uuid "f5168c4d-1a9d-5db6-b1eb-f2e01f7135ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c141ccd2-2281-55be-a7bc-e600a17abbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504361} :answer-field/id #uuid "65db8db6-731c-5056-8281-d2ccd26fd053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55ce71ca-d33d-5c8b-9d05-07e0dca2765b" :question/math-academy-id "q-152891" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}))=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}):$   \n\n![](images/4a/4abceba56f404b0b39fbde4b1f7da0ec36e69a496c4ef52d3bebfbcb8c7d3380.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$    \n\nNow, substituting $x=3$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "977816b7-4fd7-58d3-ad05-84b0516388b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504300} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cc2627-ca8c-5785-afee-c7aa3a6b9e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504351} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b3a3ba-da7b-5bc7-8ddc-f3120d8a60b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504374} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4ccd6c-60a5-5dcd-90a6-00a10987ff2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504385} {:answer/id #uuid "e717cc3f-4262-502e-ad3b-b445d589e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e717cc3f-4262-502e-ad3b-b445d589e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504431} :answer-field/id #uuid "39e0d4fd-93b9-5bf3-8ffa-8c8d4ca83b97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57542381-13e5-59f8-9ea4-61779c51f267" :question/math-academy-id "q-104854" :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has an infinite discontinuity.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & x\\le 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{x-3})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/13/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca3157f-ba62-5d76-ad5b-12e2928c4115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504057} {:answer/id #uuid "672913a8-5d68-5d5d-8a83-91ab5694722f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504213} {:answer/id #uuid "83342381-c8c1-5af1-a5c4-33a732276bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504259} {:answer/id #uuid "896c4b66-2e45-5e07-b655-87368942ef1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504270} {:answer/id #uuid "98b8e083-578f-5597-8cea-7fb0c52d5329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98b8e083-578f-5597-8cea-7fb0c52d5329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504302} :answer-field/id #uuid "264050bc-0a3b-5838-a4de-f7f48cdcac28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61f7a11b-44e6-5a13-bd4e-c02585b3aa3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-338347" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2(-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ -2{u}_{1}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bdcbb96-5ec9-5007-b256-214fc5f8397c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bdcbb96-5ec9-5007-b256-214fc5f8397c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504308} :answer-field/id #uuid "36d45e39-f71d-51b2-ae6d-2faee3a34b79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "650ff22d-86ac-5784-9e2d-04a2cd40b0db" :question/math-academy-id "q-341951" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ equals the function's derivative at the corresponding point.\n\nWe have the function \n\n$$\ny=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))+({x}^{2})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))+\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}{|}_{x=e/3} & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)}) \\\\  & =(1)^{(e/3)^{2}}(2(\\frac{e}{3})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{(\\frac{e}{3})}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0+\\frac{e}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c2c4de-610f-51d5-844f-c2ff3be490af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20c2c4de-610f-51d5-844f-c2ff3be490af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504090} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1fc9040-4cdf-577b-b242-fcc38c2ab2bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78505148-e7b2-516b-80ad-17e7629d4193" :question/math-academy-id "q-302680" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\n \n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a368caa-071a-5e09-b895-f6fee3f0b562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504217} {:answer/id #uuid "9314c4b8-7707-5373-9534-1d40448d8d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504292} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b7b665-8ee1-53eb-8f95-7a0b77e77444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504326} {:answer/id #uuid "c58ce9c8-7667-5765-b9a9-b725886916c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504366} {:answer/id #uuid "efdf1a8b-3c3d-5e6c-971a-9dd68e5b7b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9314c4b8-7707-5373-9534-1d40448d8d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504292} :answer-field/id #uuid "726c20fa-e270-57b1-9453-2ca197a81f1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88e928a1-3941-5cdc-9fa6-49a8d4b468c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-107915" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-2}, & x\\ne 4, \\\\ c, & x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4,$ what is the value of $c?$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-2}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x) & =f(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504052} {:answer/id #uuid "169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504072} {:answer/id #uuid "24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504094} {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} {:answer/id #uuid "2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130279" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4944b0a6-4855-57d9-b5a2-68a7cc64bcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4944b0a6-4855-57d9-b5a2-68a7cc64bcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504165} :answer-field/id #uuid "0935f3a5-2efe-5911-b0a9-10c3fad934c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af7b8af8-e50c-5536-8725-7ed61d38a3dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-340282" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x-1}, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either ${x}^{2}-1$ or $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of ${x}^{2}-1.$ Since ${x}^{2}-1$ is a polynomial, therefore it cannot grow without bound due to a vertical asymptote. So this function cannot have an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{x-1}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>1$, we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\frac{2}{x-1})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the only point where the function $y=f(x)$ has an infinite discontinuity is  $x=1.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cba4d1e7cab2340e24d176cee3e03c6a103cf0c69fbf15209c5f4b92a4993e2.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35dd2c60-3ba2-5d59-807e-7fa2a1be078f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35dd2c60-3ba2-5d59-807e-7fa2a1be078f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504124} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd59274-06d9-5099-a7f2-cee098896848" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8713a5b-a771-5f4a-a60c-95ad22b4fac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8713a5b-a771-5f4a-a60c-95ad22b4fac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504466} :answer-field/id #uuid "70aae3e0-86ce-5d9e-ac8d-3f5991ef70fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bccf9705-1345-5b91-b48e-5b2d9522eb17" :question/math-academy-id "q-334837" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the $x$-values  where the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-x}, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8-x}, & x\\ge 5,\\,x\\ne 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n has an infinite discontinuity are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,$ $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\frac{1}{5-x}$ or $\\frac{1}{8-x}$ grow without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{5-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n5-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x<5,$ which excludes $x=5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{5-x})=+\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{8-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n8-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $5\\le x<8$ and $x>8,$ which excludes $x=8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=\\infty \\,,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=8$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the function $y=f(x)$ has infinite discontinuities at \n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7773030c42d9e26a737ad1ed0208900101ed291189c7a1bc1430a808659ba33.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a10c49bb-a853-5a0a-96ce-acd212343d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a10c49bb-a853-5a0a-96ce-acd212343d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504317} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dc400da-c09d-518f-b91a-2b22630e2790" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdfb619c-431d-5983-938b-5503bb7b30f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-339480" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ad20156-2882-5a14-94a6-b4ce02519922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ad20156-2882-5a14-94a6-b4ce02519922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504243} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0fcb170-b0cc-55d0-ae9f-7fe8d73132e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0a1a56f-e520-52c2-bfca-2937cd7b17ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-344539" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x):$ \n\n![](images/87/873a6fb793d2ae68eab172132e467a48a43ab84733ad92155fe6cc012f516382.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3-2x))=6\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})=3\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010caea2-dc88-5554-b297-923b9c26e101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504031} {:answer/id #uuid "22702d51-f9d4-5b6f-a7d5-a2dfc890ba7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504092} {:answer/id #uuid "4573f7f6-80c8-5961-9ca6-07a1a45f8d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504155} {:answer/id #uuid "a26224f4-9c1f-5c43-83e9-54ec562ae417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504322} {:answer/id #uuid "aaeb708e-d830-5aab-aba8-919a0ff20261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{\\pi n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\pi n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4573f7f6-80c8-5961-9ca6-07a1a45f8d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504155} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d154d8e-288a-5661-8af2-ccc74136c604" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d31b1ab6-50b9-57eb-ba32-5357e77d62ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-113931" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi .$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi -0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f116d16e-87ef-5163-a0be-ec090d753eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f116d16e-87ef-5163-a0be-ec090d753eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504450} :answer-field/id #uuid "d284849d-09d1-59ac-a76f-00d1db75563f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e24c9c25-519c-53c3-84e2-e60c0348d7f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-346208" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+2)^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}+2\\,)}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5de69524-b32f-529b-907d-70d9251c90c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5de69524-b32f-529b-907d-70d9251c90c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504194} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cbd0b0a-4f04-5099-bf6e-7a48a06d53ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9eb4864-b21d-5e66-84e3-4be25804889c" :question/math-academy-id "q-341713" :question/problem "Given that $y=5e{x}^{1/x}$, find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=5e{x}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e{x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{1/x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5e)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =0+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & ={x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=5e{x}^{1/x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5e{x}^{1/x}[{x}^{-2}(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)] \\\\  & =5e{x}^{1/x-2}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c0cd33a-fca9-5c78-8f3b-efa5df68b680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504083} {:answer/id #uuid "25291edd-4784-5a6b-bca1-a12e8664f92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504098} {:answer/id #uuid "4afab431-d446-53d4-92fd-4a3980b1aff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504169} {:answer/id #uuid "59b19465-a919-5d91-b4bb-f487a9c859aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No infinite discontinuities" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504188} {:answer/id #uuid "f2286a1f-5879-59e5-8aba-c9d1177ea0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-8$ and $x=-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25291edd-4784-5a6b-bca1-a12e8664f92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504098} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a17e3a-0fbe-5957-845d-21ab2cabc3a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdd8bd57-2d2f-5ace-b80e-0473ac5a0a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-104860" :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{x+8}, & x\\ne -8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $-\\frac{1}{x+8}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{1}{x+8}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-8.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{-}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{+}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-8$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed2cae2a50cb15f86ead5831990366db2048c100d71dc9d1b681b890da38844f.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8da8f1d5-799d-5f59-b8d4-b6195a7bf724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504281} {:answer/id #uuid "98c059aa-5dc8-5f1e-bc28-ea423a043d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504303} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1b4241-9760-5288-9766-35e472eeb9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504314} {:answer/id #uuid "ceab5914-dcf2-543c-a2ec-0ff7433b8577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504384} {:answer/id #uuid "f79b5c6d-0b46-5c5d-8893-792aa3b17df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceab5914-dcf2-543c-a2ec-0ff7433b8577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504384} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f2f07fa-cb25-5801-b6e0-0205e0afe77f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffda6d3a-e6e4-5195-9f9f-69837bd2b6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338353" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′} &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) &=2x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ -2(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}+2{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ 2{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & =2x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9dcace-dd91-581c-866e-f6d1aa1b2cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504770} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb644e7-b21f-5fce-8ff0-497d610ecaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504775} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf206f0-3208-5daf-92b4-982429991a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504798} {:answer/id #uuid "8355501f-d15e-5901-913e-d30d08a04672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504800} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b2c6bf-5dbe-54b3-abdf-e2d66204b501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bb644e7-b21f-5fce-8ff0-497d610ecaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504775} :answer-field/id #uuid "64e7c313-092b-5316-b111-b5874ef1f27d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14ea91d3-f94c-5dd9-b51c-cb8c953c07f2" :question/problem "Given that $-3a - 24b$ and $λ(0.5a + 4b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b)=λ(\\frac{1}{2})a+λ(4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-3}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-24}{4} = -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-3a - 24b=-6(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3f1c2f-be82-53cd-aff4-7e5521c7167f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504749} {:answer/id #uuid "2eca6419-7234-51d4-b873-e7f3bd1bacf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504772} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4d6bea-566b-5d1a-8261-205958a929e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504784} {:answer/id #uuid "6aeefdc9-79a6-53eb-956a-d3afdf335860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504794} {:answer/id #uuid "f889768d-c9e5-5cf4-906b-9103278e26a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b4d6bea-566b-5d1a-8261-205958a929e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504784} :answer-field/id #uuid "7de5c44f-68ff-569f-b35c-cf4b217b5b0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "281e43ed-d77f-5100-ba06-3efb56464379" :question/problem "Given that $λ(0.25a - 3b)$ and $-a + 12b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{4}a - 3b)=λ(\\frac{1}{4})a+λ(-3)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{4}} = -4 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{12}{-3} = -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a + 12b=-4(\\frac{1}{4}a - 3b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-4\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d92bf8b-f09a-519e-b6c2-297a8dbbae50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504757} {:answer/id #uuid "6742f50f-0fc1-5cef-9bfc-a345a595db6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504793} {:answer/id #uuid "a142d2dd-1c1e-5f0b-b6cf-e6016f0ff340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504808} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bea5f0-8a2f-5d26-b298-26810baae035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504813} {:answer/id #uuid "fd39139a-489f-500a-bb43-0430f464ded4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bea5f0-8a2f-5d26-b298-26810baae035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504813} :answer-field/id #uuid "eac2129e-641b-514b-a9a0-d474e422aab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e813b171-b626-57db-af36-b0b2e170a174" :question/problem "Given that $λ(0.5a + 3b)$ and $-a - 6b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 3b)=λ(\\frac{1}{2})a+λ(3)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -2 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-6}{3} = -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a - 6b=-2(\\frac{1}{2}a + 3b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-2\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bd2dae-9f93-5a40-8d4b-eef040995cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504744} {:answer/id #uuid "363e5073-dc2e-5bbf-8201-fe87b22042bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504774} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2d54b2-1153-5fc4-9a0e-c048e849ed9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504777} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd349ad-f11c-561b-b033-1a228c5244e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504789} {:answer/id #uuid "b507226d-14fb-5b7f-9ec4-42ba988fbde3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fd349ad-f11c-561b-b033-1a228c5244e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504789} :answer-field/id #uuid "22efb133-43d7-5ecd-b9af-451a0d3ef57a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0dc3593-c754-5805-816a-41c4fccabc8f" :question/problem "Given that $3a + 24b$ and $λ(0.5a + 4b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b)=λ(\\frac{1}{2})a+λ(4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{3}{\\frac{1}{2}} = 6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n3a + 24b=6(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are the same because the factor is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150e0fae-7ea0-5e55-aa28-b1cf2c56b250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504754} {:answer/id #uuid "63109784-3c77-52c6-a07b-5fa548fbd809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504790} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8036ee-262d-5fb7-9adf-3a8cb2f1b4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504795} {:answer/id #uuid "8fad182c-2996-57a2-982a-7cc91e8a14de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504805} {:answer/id #uuid "bf945500-ba30-51dc-a6bf-5f492b1428ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e8036ee-262d-5fb7-9adf-3a8cb2f1b4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504795} :answer-field/id #uuid "291021b9-5572-5413-9bb4-fde113118354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0e435c8-cd66-5b3b-b59e-c0b5f48a6d99" :question/problem "Given that $-a - 2b$ and $λ(0.5a + b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + b)=λ(\\frac{1}{2})a+λ(1)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -2 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-2}{1} = -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a - 2b=-2(\\frac{1}{2}a + b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-2\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f1a14a-9931-51c6-9320-4b1b53558144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f1a14a-9931-51c6-9320-4b1b53558144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505205} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2d3b25e-e4d9-5fd1-8ed1-6f85c37a6a81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1131799e-bf6d-5253-8a7c-8f906c272bb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-341314" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=ln\\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50}{49}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50-49}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "974fd4ef-27c2-5179-8fcf-ea2529f27fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "974fd4ef-27c2-5179-8fcf-ea2529f27fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505397} :answer-field/id #uuid "e21bd3d7-6532-527f-ab53-721fcea93fbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1203a643-2d1d-5bdd-a410-353b4bd6d719" :question/math-academy-id "q-342289" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}),$ for $m>0.$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16-8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16+8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+|\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $m>0,$ we have $\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{m}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7a9afd-62a6-5b93-b7a2-483a714de544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a7a9afd-62a6-5b93-b7a2-483a714de544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505254} :answer-field/id #uuid "4354c19a-ced8-5d91-8370-a595dbdb9d13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5baf8c7a-819a-521e-a261-67b2db30681d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341486" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})}{1-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{\\frac{5+\\sqrt{3}}{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,ln\\,(\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0302529d-68d6-501c-b539-7e8521229961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2-y}{2+y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505110} {:answer/id #uuid "570713fe-220d-50d8-94c9-2619c6bc8a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2y-1}{2y+1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505280} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc6266d-b0e7-541f-bf6f-0b745cd929ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y-2}{y+2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505527} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc3d996-dd1b-572c-8d28-9f09efecaae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y+2}{y-2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505533} {:answer/id #uuid "ed795175-c482-546e-8a40-d08968c9f851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+y}{2-y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0302529d-68d6-501c-b539-7e8521229961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2-y}{2+y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505110} :answer-field/id #uuid "021f330a-b40b-5b0d-8fd5-b8401e6cfa50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "670d700d-b88f-53f5-a139-73bdf8c93272" :question/math-academy-id "q-126259" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $y\\in (-2,2),$ which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{y}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{y}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{y}{2})}{1-(-\\frac{y}{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{y}{2}}{1+\\frac{y}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{2-y}{2}}{\\frac{2+y}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2-y}{2+y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc5eff9-4511-5ebc-9346-fef6d3ff2cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505293} {:answer/id #uuid "61a52828-b97a-55bc-89af-abdc326b7088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505301} {:answer/id #uuid "a747a90c-e5d0-5aef-9c1b-23569a104c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505440} {:answer/id #uuid "bf18c3df-1eff-5189-830c-ed12cf41dde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505476} {:answer/id #uuid "cae550b1-5979-52c8-970e-b1b797158855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a747a90c-e5d0-5aef-9c1b-23569a104c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505440} :answer-field/id #uuid "7876217b-a5c3-5d52-8219-aba955692660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6b6577b4-d24d-5962-9f5c-436ba639df8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73371" :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{(\\frac{2}{1})}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "521f1d1c-c4a9-54ce-be0a-26a5043158cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "521f1d1c-c4a9-54ce-be0a-26a5043158cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505268} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dc0e4ad-a39d-502a-8eb5-af62880a7035" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70bc61eb-58b7-5349-9bef-f900f312799d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342615" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ x & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e56c770a-5806-5999-8f0f-05f21b960997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{p}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56c770a-5806-5999-8f0f-05f21b960997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{p}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505553} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2227fe-2623-5005-877d-8e648618036c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b0ffba6-735a-5d4b-89a4-021c8a5b9247" :question/math-academy-id "q-343590" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $p\\ge 25,$ express $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic cosine.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}+50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}-50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+|\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p\\ge 25,$ we have $\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{p}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d086c220-4627-5c91-b9c1-4b6c8a97c4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d086c220-4627-5c91-b9c1-4b6c8a97c4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505504} :answer-field/id #uuid "27bd1c0c-89e6-510d-8840-4d12f9be8883" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505627} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f6aaefd-82b2-597b-94e2-766881cdd3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f6aaefd-82b2-597b-94e2-766881cdd3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505331} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6590ac0-071c-5dc7-835a-5e92e0475c4a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505684} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e303b12d-c9eb-52a4-877a-c6da28a1a9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e303b12d-c9eb-52a4-877a-c6da28a1a9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505547} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1127e0f-95b4-5a48-b495-9387fefcb90d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "821d20a5-8c94-5b58-8ee1-508076709a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-351917" :question/problem "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-x}$ about $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$(x-\\frac{1}{2})\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we plug $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(\\frac{1}{2})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}){(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(\\frac{1}{2}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{1-x} & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(\\frac{1}{2}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+8{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba3e473-02fb-5157-b482-3554be83ad62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505185} {:answer/id #uuid "45918914-cbad-5fa0-8b0f-566e642a966c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505246} {:answer/id #uuid "5762675e-b9b2-5628-ae14-b2f8bf8c8a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505281} {:answer/id #uuid "68bef3d6-95b9-53bc-bfd3-a4201accc046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505316} {:answer/id #uuid "ef341ed2-5b5f-5140-8092-d50bef9018a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ba3e473-02fb-5157-b482-3554be83ad62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505185} :answer-field/id #uuid "67fc1eae-c999-56ca-96a1-f0623bb0224c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83ade4bd-cffc-596f-9566-1eb5e39170cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-95616" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{1}{4})}{1-(-\\frac{1}{4})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{1}{4}}{1+\\frac{1}{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}}{\\frac{5}{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e1275fc-45d5-56e9-921c-419284dfd186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505260} {:answer/id #uuid "64b7ad1d-e8f4-5ece-9cdb-d0b6a18c31ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+6}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505307} {:answer/id #uuid "70ac23c1-c539-56dc-ab99-2f9ae1aa9224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+\\sqrt{6}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505335} {:answer/id #uuid "c6970587-5e60-5326-ba2f-20db18796990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6-\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505490} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd92dee-655a-57ac-b2c7-90a821947212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e1275fc-45d5-56e9-921c-419284dfd186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505260} :answer-field/id #uuid "7107c43c-bc1f-5f63-9a1c-c16d62962b34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "854f76b3-c5a6-5b21-803f-68a8249b452b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9601" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c693b7-cccd-550b-a54d-0a489e678e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{9}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505119} {:answer/id #uuid "0c559b12-c2b6-5701-81a8-9ef77e5ea06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505126} {:answer/id #uuid "30e5f82b-22e6-5d14-87b0-25b92fa198e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505189} {:answer/id #uuid "9a953977-58ca-5907-ba22-17c28ab4e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505406} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6d3902-4c49-563c-8ffd-9bc430647bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a953977-58ca-5907-ba22-17c28ab4e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505406} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bbba225-3f98-5236-a2d6-45d639d898aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ce8f55a-871b-590a-9437-e579e7e6b7ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-125838" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{9}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d786de3f-c166-5197-8b45-69ebfef00b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d786de3f-c166-5197-8b45-69ebfef00b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505521} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7beb05c-ce8a-51e7-be2b-4e84b5896568" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5800963-d7c6-5c24-9f66-ca5ef86b26b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-341494" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $0<x<\\frac{1}{4},$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+4\\sqrt{x}-1}{1-4\\sqrt{x}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{2-4\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e28eb08e-4a02-5921-830b-171458c8d0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{400}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e28eb08e-4a02-5921-830b-171458c8d0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{400}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505545} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d270b7c-49c4-5539-9b50-53390b12221d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b66642ed-0ee4-55e3-841c-23e21fdfce5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166818" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction, the exact amount of paint available is$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters." :question/worked-solution "The exact amount of paint is ${(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}$ liters.\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{20}{9}\\times \\frac{20}{9}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\times 20}{9\\times 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{400}{81}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57752369-ee0f-585d-9307-31648dc6aed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505282} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf67a66-1f7d-589b-9794-976a6ee03856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505324} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6725ad-f38e-5393-96b7-7603ac0c2f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505329} {:answer/id #uuid "a9187687-7c45-54ed-92e1-7d05ac532cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505442} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f34fb5-2b1a-527d-8cbd-18a361eec573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3f34fb5-2b1a-527d-8cbd-18a361eec573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505455} :answer-field/id #uuid "9163b72a-ca3f-56d5-be84-6c361f7f2037" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcf19778-fecb-5e86-ab67-3237ac262859" :question/math-academy-id "q-73357" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ | $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5999c02b-4e0a-5088-a1ce-199924772d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+w}{1-w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5999c02b-4e0a-5088-a1ce-199924772d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+w}{1-w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505285} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c578e06-f08e-5dc7-b0a7-5b75a8b2c043" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c284afac-baa7-5076-92a3-0d89b2214f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-341500" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $-2<w<1,$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+\\frac{2w+1}{3}}{1-\\frac{2w+1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3+2w+1}{3}}{\\frac{3-2w-1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4+2w}{2-2w}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+w}{1-w}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2520222e-c4e3-55e1-9c0c-b0256d5a01c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-x}{1+x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505167} {:answer/id #uuid "6325d0ab-7ae4-5d74-ad04-8971639df056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505304} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4eebfc-75b0-57eb-acfb-cae4a0c06d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505327} {:answer/id #uuid "cec8de79-540b-5e73-9891-cd653f228d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505501} {:answer/id #uuid "d5fe4910-17a2-5f7b-b1a8-694b1e285a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-x}{1+x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e4eebfc-75b0-57eb-acfb-cae4a0c06d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505327} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b4efcc4-5cc4-5cf8-81d6-746ced2d80e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2e8309b-0e6c-591c-8179-3fd8b7ab6d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-125981" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡({x}^{2})$  and $x\\ge 0,$ calculate  ${f}^{-1}(x)$ from first-principles." :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on $x\\ge 0.$ We have $y\\in [0,1)$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,1)$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1}{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1} \\\\ x({e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1) & ={e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1 \\\\ (x-1){e}^{2{y}^{2}} & +(x+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the ${e}^{2{y}^{2}}$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{x+1}{x-1} \\\\ {e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{1+x}{1-x} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we select the positive root. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60753404-5bba-5ada-91bb-6605d38b24e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60753404-5bba-5ada-91bb-6605d38b24e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505299} :answer-field/id #uuid "99bf3307-f793-5f62-81bd-770491a15c81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfca6b72-2e68-5c63-8b6b-82019963b193" :question/math-academy-id "q-351931" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+2}=(x+2)^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-(x+2)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(x+2)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=2(1+2)^{-3}=\\frac{2}{27}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}=\\frac{(\\frac{2}{27})}{2}=\\frac{1}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5976a6b-1d1d-57ce-8564-63c89f695029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5976a6b-1d1d-57ce-8564-63c89f695029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505555} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5cb8799-b859-5b82-be20-c6134d8d1332" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e2444a41-f0c0-50e5-91b8-38bd6aeb8a43" :question/math-academy-id "q-347942" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{18}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{(\\frac{2}{1})}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210bc91a-0886-5112-af4a-e207417a27a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{2})t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505159} {:answer/id #uuid "42566f74-ff91-595b-a775-feb8083bfe0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505236} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff4e27e-6481-5c9b-833b-fc99bca024b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505298} {:answer/id #uuid "75971b4e-945e-5996-88b2-e5e64c3d4697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505344} {:answer/id #uuid "f4709c83-7348-56c6-8fb5-f7518f7b7264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ff4e27e-6481-5c9b-833b-fc99bca024b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505298} :answer-field/id #uuid "f35fe716-2eba-5add-9591-f3a00949c2d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdaedc69-27ce-51b2-8939-51c5e1dbf0b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125843" :question/problem "$$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}),$ for $t>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+|\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we have $\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2{t}^{2}}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b15c20-ae96-5510-bdf6-238327aebb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505977} {:answer/id #uuid "9420b75f-9ddc-56f2-8501-2ed81e404fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506163} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa83dc4-ef7c-5fb9-9ca5-7ad2f609a832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506198} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa37242-7820-582d-b32a-092b97e43ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506282} {:answer/id #uuid "f9347296-ee2c-5eec-91b9-fff04cdeed07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27b15c20-ae96-5510-bdf6-238327aebb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505977} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b45ba0-9d41-52b1-8d31-cba0ed77bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06abf62c-6dfa-52f6-818c-3858ac967b34" :question/math-academy-id "q-138850" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((ax+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(b{x}^{2}+cx)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{36(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((36x+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(1{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+1+2=39.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39eac370-b597-5b13-a139-ed248af8a490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506009} {:answer/id #uuid "3e37cd6e-52b0-57de-acb7-d9a7a4ef0fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506014} {:answer/id #uuid "6c234f23-48fe-556c-a0b1-24c477bfbdfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506100} {:answer/id #uuid "d2bacdde-7997-50ac-944f-7a50e4a49e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506288} {:answer/id #uuid "deac3608-20ae-52c0-97b0-276c7fcb4d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2bacdde-7997-50ac-944f-7a50e4a49e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506288} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3dff71e-7253-5db5-a8db-526f8787e942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11c48698-c6fd-5725-a4b0-1737e6fdc309" :question/math-academy-id "q-138831" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((ax+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+6)^{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({cos}^{4}⁡x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3(x-1)-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(x+6)(x-1)-7(x+6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{-4x-45-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}(4x+45+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(4{x}^{2}+20x-24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+20+(-24)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1424a6ad-ac38-52fa-bea7-f95d3db15e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14x^2+63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1424a6ad-ac38-52fa-bea7-f95d3db15e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14x^2+63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505950} :answer-field/id #uuid "61e10619-0457-5f47-8c3c-d968af092244" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19b1b664-4a0c-5ebc-8d57-f886d4c9f0c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-343952" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}=$ | $-$ | $x($\n{{field-1}}$)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\sqrt[4]{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}]}^{1/4} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot (2x)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot (4x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{x}{2({x}^{2}+4)}-\\frac{8x}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{x(2{x}^{2}+1)-16x({x}^{2}+4)}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{-14{x}^{3}-63x}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x(14{x}^{2}+63)}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\sqrt[4]{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fc9c0e4-94e6-5a58-b402-1569ccdf0546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=-4$ and $z=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506114} {:answer/id #uuid "854c086c-10d6-59fc-a728-734ed635b82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=-\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506144} {:answer/id #uuid "9dedfe61-c595-5e0a-ab8d-634757cce0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{25}{4},\\,z=-4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506189} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b96d21-d357-5674-892a-42feaf9e8b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{3}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506208} {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb231a-0f38-50bf-8cf9-3768fa9ab226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb231a-0f38-50bf-8cf9-3768fa9ab226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506356} :answer-field/id #uuid "9418bd8b-7dee-502f-b277-9872940fd25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2236615a-502f-5e6f-8a3b-d0bb2e05f4c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-35049" :question/problem "The equation $3{z}^{2}-12=9z$ can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-12 & =9z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-9z-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-3z-4) & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-3z-4 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} & =4 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ [{z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}]-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\frac{3}{2},q=\\frac{25}{4}$ and $p+q=\\frac{19}{4}.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |z-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}=4 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=4$ and $z=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "419792fe-6389-5363-a771-125381c66945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x^3+8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "419792fe-6389-5363-a771-125381c66945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x^3+8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506021} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cca9dd-6ad8-57a8-a947-88e8f34aa52d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "254041d3-aef5-5074-80f4-67b8b1344bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-312672" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}($\n{{field-1}}$+\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8))$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot (4{x}^{3}+4x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}+4x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8>0\\text{ }∀x\\in R$, so $|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}[\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot ({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(8{x}^{3}+8x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8))\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ebc9a01-e27f-509b-a68a-42b56dc2ad7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\ln(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ebc9a01-e27f-509b-a68a-42b56dc2ad7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\ln(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506045} :answer-field/id #uuid "7487540a-070b-5d24-9ba2-dc636972e043" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506432} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec54cd01-acca-5a25-833a-60eab750baf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec54cd01-acca-5a25-833a-60eab750baf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506345} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0efc8ad-b4c8-5f06-a724-3416448817ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506453} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97af42c5-8f26-50fc-a29e-fb1a80f1a04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97af42c5-8f26-50fc-a29e-fb1a80f1a04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506175} :answer-field/id #uuid "d150bf24-0895-5aa8-98a7-c4a97ebe264b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "310e9298-b624-54d9-ac03-bed77b7fdecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343350" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}.$  Find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | ${6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,($\n{{field-1}}$(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x-2)^{5}(3-x)^{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient, product, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3-x)^{3})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}-3\\cdot \\frac{-1}{3-x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4(3-x)+3(x-2)}{(x-2)(3-x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18}{(x-2)(3-x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18)}{(x-2)^{5}(3-x)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e5c2fc-62c3-5cff-8c3f-12578ac7eec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505922} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd9e9ba-35f5-5dc8-b23d-1b83df5ca67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506037} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad7d797-78b2-528c-9d9b-806dc635caa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506068} {:answer/id #uuid "8c834b34-6910-5d98-ab6c-76660ea17f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506154} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ab0c31-9934-5df4-8de8-4658ed227b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3ab0c31-9934-5df4-8de8-4658ed227b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506207} :answer-field/id #uuid "893ce6e9-d2a5-5687-890a-43b20d335c93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3340fda4-31bb-56af-bac8-5dcbd67440f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-116730" :question/problem "A spherical hot air balloon is inflated. At a particular moment, the rate of increase in the volume of the balloon is numerically equal to nine-halfs the rate of increase in its surface area. What is the radius of the balloon at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{(\\frac{A}{4\\pi })}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =18\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & ={(18\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2} \\\\ A & =324\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =324\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =81 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7482730a-2ef8-51f5-a35d-2f7208beda4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7482730a-2ef8-51f5-a35d-2f7208beda4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506120} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d1d24c-d1f3-5b29-bd9f-d25502738b67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37194e35-397f-5f0f-b8ce-0e1f1331cb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344726" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c16496b13f9ad9fc995cc59dedf1777f25e394ca61dd2ea493d8bd8f69c5e14.png)\n\nPaint dripping onto a floor forms a circular puddle. The radius of the puddle increases at a rate of $10\\,\\text{in/min}.$ How fast is the area of the puddle increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{9}{4\\pi }\\,\\text{in}?$\n\nThe area of the puddle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10$ and $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =10\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{9}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the puddle is increasing at a rate of $45\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d9ae4e-8ee9-5dab-a678-998b84633eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/1443d401eb2bd5a6a699f6c80fdd33f95270bed6e24d55165aff7b90bbd546b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505947} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9ef012-bf37-5052-b96d-18f78871459b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/ebda9e955c2613aa9e65b950ac80ad7ec4e52d6db6fdcce76cc2f89bbc20b0d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505993} {:answer/id #uuid "364c3910-7df3-5411-bad0-d653131067da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d2e6671729b92d2ac42a357268a6545f1d3491c7c1a6133e7e460227c8a04da.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506003} {:answer/id #uuid "93ee5e59-9e5d-5c6a-8a09-ad948704dae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/35/3589e407d3ff892f4bfb00e73cf0c5e77a7fb4aaee39ecc3daeca53b60b49773.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506162} {:answer/id #uuid "99281185-8a83-594a-94a5-070bba47ed34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/944ad59d879c9eb7fbf08615659d5ac6a4b88ca37cb2dda50ccad7aa3f28046b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d9ef012-bf37-5052-b96d-18f78871459b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/ebda9e955c2613aa9e65b950ac80ad7ec4e52d6db6fdcce76cc2f89bbc20b0d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505993} :answer-field/id #uuid "f308d5d5-0b30-5d3f-8d6d-a8777cb225e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f4cf73b-e442-544c-845a-c7e6521bdf69" :question/math-academy-id "q-155556" :question/problem "Represent the solution of the inequality $-\\frac{a}{4}>-2$ on a number line." :question/worked-solution "The variable $a$ is being *divided* by $4.$ To isolate $a$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-4.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{a}{4}\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-2 \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4})\\,\\, & <\\,\\,(-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4})\\,\\, & <\\,\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4}) & <8 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}4)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{a}{4}) & <8 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{a}{4} & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{a}{4} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{4a}{4} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}a}{\\cancel{4}} & <8 \\\\ a & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a<8.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/eb/ebda9e955c2613aa9e65b950ac80ad7ec4e52d6db6fdcce76cc2f89bbc20b0d4.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d50330c-784c-5a84-ac1e-5c7c5be92530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{13}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505935} {:answer/id #uuid "2393a4d4-762d-5560-9226-5a0e216dec51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505969} {:answer/id #uuid "8910933f-a80b-5b82-961d-2627b5f5280c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506150} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ee5ca9-ea89-54cd-839a-29262cd0a71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x=-1$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506213} {:answer/id #uuid "f62dae0d-aa09-5a1e-b79c-fc4344431af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2393a4d4-762d-5560-9226-5a0e216dec51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505969} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb7d856-8042-5bbe-be63-7001e1b25a2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6c6b5462-ae7e-592e-8c21-3452425e7810" :question/math-academy-id "q-60363" :question/problem "The equation $-4{x}^{2}-6x+10=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-6x+10) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =10 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =10 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =10+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}=-\\frac{10}{4}=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c28e5d-0de9-502d-81aa-7351f4920069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505924} {:answer/id #uuid "90c27865-1c0b-519b-81bb-de523a0ce9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506157} {:answer/id #uuid "93851dca-79be-57a5-b496-18ef7187487f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506161} {:answer/id #uuid "b193db5a-f715-5820-8744-ed3301ee43c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506234} {:answer/id #uuid "ebacda43-d38a-518b-8124-0083b7f84186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebacda43-d38a-518b-8124-0083b7f84186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506342} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d436c14-aa02-5c1c-8f96-e30ae5edad2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76085196-6a2f-5c76-90cb-5c742aa98a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-138849" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}(x-3)^{3}(a{x}^{2}+bx+c).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(x-3)^{8}} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{8/2} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-3)^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{3x(x-3)+4({x}^{2}+1)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{7{x}^{2}-9x+4}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}(\\frac{7{x}^{2}-9x+4}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{1/2}(x-3)^{3}(7{x}^{2}-9x+4) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}(x-3)^{3}(7{x}^{2}-9x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=7+(-9)+4=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102790ff-7839-5d1c-a7c1-c89c090c0d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "102790ff-7839-5d1c-a7c1-c89c090c0d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505942} :answer-field/id #uuid "274547bb-be34-5b65-bb9d-f32876fabd37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506398} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4323142-e936-5cd3-b6de-3092378faa67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4323142-e936-5cd3-b6de-3092378faa67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506359} :answer-field/id #uuid "94665d5f-2175-540b-accc-e742e344e1b0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506448} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5274db64-5e9a-58d1-a778-b4f7735c7525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5274db64-5e9a-58d1-a778-b4f7735c7525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506050} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03d9a12-bbbf-5ee1-a420-92022427414d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a442047-ed6e-5bb4-89b9-beaee4b1e553" :question/math-academy-id "q-338291" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}.$  Find the derivative of $y.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | $(x+2)^{3}\\,(-5{x}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{2}\\,+$ \n{{field-2}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+2)^{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+2x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{3(3{x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{3}+2x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{4({x}^{3}+2x-1)-(x+2)(9{x}^{2}+6)}{(x+2)({x}^{3}+2x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{-5{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+2x-16}{(x+2)({x}^{3}+2x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{3}(-5{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+2x-16)}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24dcf1bc-40b1-5496-a498-3a7dae4ffe85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "271" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505972} {:answer/id #uuid "94c691dd-0578-5e12-9cc1-90a7a7bb4085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506165} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f872ba-2453-55d3-97e1-d1431eaa6668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506204} {:answer/id #uuid "e2265b1b-5fa7-5e22-a840-d2ec09a5ad1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506326} {:answer/id #uuid "eef1c07b-39fe-56ff-ad05-22dd4b396724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eef1c07b-39fe-56ff-ad05-22dd4b396724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506349} :answer-field/id #uuid "13bb00dc-f20f-5ab2-8e37-6185dcb84a29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adde1803-10ec-5c3d-8c04-d7e2c5d99587" :question/math-academy-id "q-138830" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(ax+63-8({x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+8)^{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{8}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+8}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6(x+8)+5(x+3)-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x(x+3)(x+8)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6x+48+5x+15-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=11+11+24=46.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab36fdc-7bd5-5430-8835-a0815a440065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505956} {:answer/id #uuid "6d88a707-e10c-5afc-bea5-52cb9dd63b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506103} {:answer/id #uuid "7e987b10-51ae-5db1-b678-922798639d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506131} {:answer/id #uuid "9515e64c-16bf-58d0-b027-ae01ea34515a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506166} {:answer/id #uuid "d0164b4c-0fc5-5386-8a13-5df40b0b0015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0164b4c-0fc5-5386-8a13-5df40b0b0015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506284} :answer-field/id #uuid "de6a18ff-6676-56f5-b05e-7d4da313f1e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc8a78e5-ffc8-5ad6-abfc-11fb82c73f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-138865" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{a{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+b{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+c}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{3/2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2}}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2}}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x+2)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2x}\\cdot (4{x}^{3}+2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{(2{x}^{3}+1)({x}^{2}+2x+2)-(x+1)({x}^{4}+2x)}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{2{x}^{5}+4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+2x+2-({x}^{5}+{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+2x)}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{3/2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+4+2=7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202f1ef1-7013-51df-9e16-c9120bd4144f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505965} {:answer/id #uuid "5e31ff2b-eed8-54a4-9c63-1385779eeb3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506077} {:answer/id #uuid "838261f2-b5a9-5711-a5d7-9938944a3fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506140} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e52d3b-1bd1-557a-a8af-a88e19d06865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506209} {:answer/id #uuid "ddadc2d4-3fc4-5e73-bbc6-8b7b6bb74343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "838261f2-b5a9-5711-a5d7-9938944a3fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506140} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ac868f5-8945-532d-ae24-0a2c85097e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c693f3f3-9251-58ce-8006-d594413ed6b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-8010" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2}=1$ intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x,$ we first replace $y$ with $\\frac{1}{3}x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the last equation is a false statement, we conclude that the hyperbola does not intersect the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f09fc632-c551-511d-b5ff-0a2ef83dfc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f09fc632-c551-511d-b5ff-0a2ef83dfc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506354} :answer-field/id #uuid "70fdc4f0-4261-5d00-b2fa-9b5690965424" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9d699d5-e68c-517c-8c66-c0218f7be321" :question/math-academy-id "q-344727" :question/problem "![](images/95/95e09441c0ab651a759862d3689aee517d089a43d2089697a1b77882de1e5d73.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a patio forms a circular pool. The radius of the pool increases at a rate of $3\\,\\text{m/min}.$ How fast is the area of the pool increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{m}?$\n\nThe area of the pool is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{5}{2\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the pool is increasing at a rate of $15\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f59a9ba-364e-508e-a41a-55a558b2bb49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505941} {:answer/id #uuid "478822c2-e030-5e90-884b-19998c062866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506029} {:answer/id #uuid "75b16ee9-4f9f-5d41-b9b6-01fc0fada42c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506121} {:answer/id #uuid "9e556be2-fc3f-5682-8727-573dea82908b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506192} {:answer/id #uuid "cb83d552-a42d-5c11-aa25-bd36f59bc3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f59a9ba-364e-508e-a41a-55a558b2bb49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505941} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b209a8-1c66-5330-a9ec-0e55e08cba8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbe4c4ed-7bf7-5234-8564-800989eb5aa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-138839" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}((x+2)^{2}+g(x)).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+1)^{{x}^{2}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-4x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-(2-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2+4x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+1}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(\\frac{2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4(x+1)}{x+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4(x+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4x+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+(x+2)^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22440f7b-035c-540b-b0e2-3dcd53956334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506747} {:answer/id #uuid "24264748-c5f8-54b5-aa97-cacdbd3e54a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506756} {:answer/id #uuid "9798202a-5866-5647-b043-5b3551c6a2a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507008} {:answer/id #uuid "baca3216-958b-5e79-b70f-2b61c19055ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507089} {:answer/id #uuid "c1195dcf-977f-50d8-932c-4e76e5fdb1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24264748-c5f8-54b5-aa97-cacdbd3e54a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506756} :answer-field/id #uuid "b73f9e06-0243-5c21-9043-a7def06f5f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14f47d14-d694-51ea-abc7-d932943c0b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-44172" :question/problem "Factor ${x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2b3346-7673-59b0-8162-666645b193ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b2b3346-7673-59b0-8162-666645b193ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506854} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b0ffb3a-4b6c-5434-a8c2-b27219cc6547" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f0168e5-5e69-5504-a3b6-aca93e900a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344924" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1d974b84be93c1178820f27c40f7b2bfae66837f5f64dcb7353f968abad966c.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(1)=2$ and $g(1)=1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,3)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (0,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3baa451a-05ef-5ea6-9e67-38333ef4f803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506819} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad18f48-f87f-5a8e-83ca-7b7baaf8d85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge 14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506892} {:answer/id #uuid "9976ab5e-8eac-58d3-a410-a11083f1fd8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507012} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ea89bc-8d49-5eb8-bdf1-af8685391da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le 14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507027} {:answer/id #uuid "ece206a6-bb4a-5ca0-a27c-6ac1106fcba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9976ab5e-8eac-58d3-a410-a11083f1fd8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507012} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd44771e-0700-538a-9998-e17199c5831d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37bbccda-0b14-5e04-9a10-40c81c14c83e" :question/math-academy-id "q-156048" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2y-8}{3}\\le \\frac{8-2y}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y-8}{3} & \\le \\frac{8-2y}{4} \\\\ 12\\cdot (\\frac{2y-8}{3}) & \\le 12\\cdot (\\frac{8-2y}{4}) \\\\ 4\\cdot (2y-8) & \\le 3\\cdot (8-2y) \\\\ 8y-32 & \\le 24-6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y-32 & \\le 24-6y \\\\ 8y-32+6y & \\le 24-6y+6y \\\\ 14y-32 & \\le 24 \\\\ 14y-32+32 & \\le 24+32 \\\\ 14y & \\le 56 \\\\ \\frac{14y}{14} & \\le \\frac{56}{14} \\\\ y & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\le 4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9b0d25f-a9be-59cb-9e73-c90c91605ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9b0d25f-a9be-59cb-9e73-c90c91605ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507052} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3c02df-5522-58ab-abd4-e9b78610a514" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507295} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdcb9fbd-5555-5112-b178-4b472e36dad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdcb9fbd-5555-5112-b178-4b472e36dad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507126} :answer-field/id #uuid "719477eb-00fb-512e-85cd-7a35def7de91" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41e3b8c9-bf8c-5f28-a82e-0ab2c9c99da3" :question/math-academy-id "q-181364" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{1-7x}{5}\\ge 2.$\n\nThe solution is $x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-7x}{5} & \\ge 2 \\\\ 5\\cdot (\\frac{1-7x}{5}) & \\ge 5\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot (\\frac{1-7x}{\\cancel{5}}) & \\ge 10 \\\\ 1-7x & \\ge 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-7x & \\ge 10 \\\\ 1-7x-1 & \\ge 10-1 \\\\ -7x & \\ge 9 \\\\ \\frac{-7x}{-7} & \\,\\,\\le \\,\\,\\frac{9}{-7} \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le -\\frac{9}{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31315a0d-2c0e-5f94-a1ff-4b7faeb27a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506794} {:answer/id #uuid "3701d3f2-5410-556e-b0a7-75420b9f36b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506810} {:answer/id #uuid "374322ff-4086-5b83-87ee-c3122f61bd52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+3)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506811} {:answer/id #uuid "c0be764b-beb0-5776-8b10-64f11f6978bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507101} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c7dbd4-3ec1-54d0-8413-03bba2ac8751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31315a0d-2c0e-5f94-a1ff-4b7faeb27a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506794} :answer-field/id #uuid "8adb6eba-9169-5d39-b01b-4fde7ef436d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e023823-cd56-5877-8c6f-e730b2d09ff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-78924" :question/problem "${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}=$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
